      "text": "No. 17-1625"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Supreme Court",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0000-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:08 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Mr. Perry, I -- I understand all that case law. I think your adversary would argue, number one, that full costs in the Copyright Act predated -- predated both the definitional inclusion of costs in the federal statutes and that it had a history, a meaning, independent of what happened later."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0000-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KEDEM: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: This Court has treated \"costs\" as a term of art defined by the list in Section 1920. That formula helps courts and litigants navigate more than 200 statutes that use the term. It gives Congress a clear baseline against which to legislate and respects history because federal law has always defined by statute the costs that could be shifted between parties in cases at common law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Well, in 1831, according to your argument, federal law didn't really care -- didn't -- didn't have its own definition of what a cost would be. It -- costs were whatever the states regarded as costs, right?\nMR. KEDEM: It wasn't a definitional provision.\nJUSTICE ALITO: Whatever it -- was in the -- and so, if I looked at every single one of the state fee schedules in 1831, what would I find?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Kedem",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KEDEM: It wasn't a definitional provision."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: -- you agree that if the word \"full\" wasn't in the statute you would lose?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: What do you say to, is there -- is there anything that will help me, and I might be unique in this, but I -- I often think that Congress when it uses these words doesn't really think about it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Clement",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The committee, if you believe legislative history at all, very much wants to provide for the recovery of \"investigatory fees,\" is the term he uses. Now they want to make sure that those are shifted to the prevailing party, and they think to themselves: Are they covered by the language we've used? And the language they've used is \"full costs.\" And the senator concludes, duh, of course they're covered. We're using \"full costs.\" How can they possibly not be covered?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Well, what if the -- what if the objection is that the attorneys' fees were outrageous considering how much was at stake in the case? Can the judge, when he's dealing with a provision about full costs, say, well, but that's just -- these fees are outrageous; I'm only going to give you half of your attorneys' fees?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under the construction just proposed, expert witness fees were mandatory in every copyright case from 1831 to 1976. Expert witness fees were actually awarded in zero copyright cases from 1831 to 1976. I will leave the Court to draw its own conclusion from that fact."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And that date, 1976, is critical because 1975 was a watershed moment in the -- in the area we're talking about. That was the Alyeska decision, Your Honor, where this Court dealt with fees and costs and expenses and said we, the Supreme Court, are going to get out of the business of -- of rewriting the rules in every case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And in 1976, the same day that Section 505 was enacted, Congress enacted four other statutes that expressly authorized expert witness fees."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But the majority in every one of those cases said cost is a term of art, and there is a clear statement rule. And Congress can override it by stating explicitly."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And our point has not been that attorneys' fees were never awarded. It's that non-scheduled fees were never included in full costs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1625",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b60c8f5c247739829e9bfa74b4e74bdf83b2c5b423bc7e3ef8a966c6292e741a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b60c8f5c2477-0003-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "421 U.S. at 271. \"It is not for this Court to invade the legislature's province by redistributing litigation costs in the manner suggested by Respondents and followed by the court of appeals.\""
    },
